A commonly used signed JWT is the compact JWS form `header.payload.signature`, with each part base64url-encoded. The payload is [[r:not encrypted]] unless a separate JWE design is used.

- Header: a pinned `alg`, a rotation key ID `kid`, and an explicit `typ` such as the access-token profile expected by the API.
- Claims: `iss`, `sub`, `aud`, `exp`, `iat`, optionally `nbf`/`jti`, and authorization data such as scopes.
- Signature: integrity/authenticity over the encoded header and payload using a trusted key.

<pre><code><span class="c">// Validation order; use a maintained JOSE/OAuth library, not home-grown crypto</span>
<span class="f">requireExpectedTypAndAllowedAlgorithm</span>(header);
Key key = trustedJwks.<span class="f">resolve</span>(header.kid());
Claims c = jws.<span class="f">verifyThenDecode</span>(key);
<span class="f">requireIssuer</span>(c, EXPECTED_ISSUER);
<span class="f">requireAudience</span>(c, THIS_API);
<span class="f">requireTimeWindow</span>(c.nbf(), c.exp(), ALLOWED_CLOCK_SKEW);
<span class="f">requireScopesAndSubjectPolicy</span>(c);</code></pre>

:::warn Parsing is not validation
Do not authorize from unverified claims. Reject `alg: none`, algorithm/key-type confusion, unexpected `typ`, untrusted `jku`/`x5u`, missing issuer/audience/time claims, and tokens intended for another API or token class. An ID token is not an API access token.
:::

:::deep
JWKS handling is production logic: fetch only from issuer configuration you trust; cache keys; rate-limit refresh on an unknown `kid`; retain previous keys through the maximum token lifetime; and overlap old/new signing keys during rotation. A naïve “refetch on every unknown kid” path is a denial-of-service vector, while a cache that never refreshes breaks rotation.

`typ`, issuer, audience, key set, and validation rules together prevent cross-JWT and token-substitution attacks. Signature validity says who signed bytes; authorization still has to check tenant, subject, scope/role, resource ownership, and current policy.

Keep claims minimal, never put secrets in a readable payload, never log the token, and choose access-token lifetime from replay risk and revocation capability rather than a universal number of minutes.
:::

- An [[b:access token]] is presented to a resource server and should be audience-restricted, least-privilege, and short enough for the replay/revocation risk.
- A [[b:refresh token]] is a higher-value credential used only at the authorization server. Issuance and lifetime are policy decisions; inactivity, consent, credential, device, or risk changes can terminate the grant early.

For public clients, RFC 9700 requires refresh tokens to be [[g:sender-constrained]] (for example DPoP) or rotated with reuse detection. Rotation issues a new token on every refresh, invalidates the predecessor, and revokes the active family when reuse reveals likely theft. Confidential clients must authenticate and the server must bind the token to that client.

Storage follows the platform threat model: a BFF keeps OAuth tokens server-side and uses a Secure/HttpOnly/SameSite cookie plus CSRF defense; native apps use OS-protected storage; browser-only apps minimize token lifetime and commonly keep access tokens in memory. HttpOnly prevents JavaScript reads, not requests made by an XSS payload, so CSP and output safety still matter.

:::deep
Revocation is layered:

- revoke the authorization/refresh-token family on logout, consent withdrawal, account compromise, credential reset, or reuse detection;
- use opaque access tokens with introspection when immediate central revocation outweighs the availability/latency cost, or a short-lived signed token when local validation is the better trade-off;
- use a `jti` denylist only for bounded emergency cases with an explicit retention/availability design;
- require reauthentication or step-up with WebAuthn/MFA for high-risk operations, even when a token is valid;
- consider DPoP or mTLS sender-constrained access tokens where bearer-token replay is a material risk.

Never log tokens or URLs/headers containing them. Redact at ingestion, restrict security-log access, and alert on refresh reuse without recording the credential itself (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q4).
:::

Prefer [[g:no long-lived secret]] when workload identity or short-lived federation can provide the credential. When a secret is necessary, keep it out of source, build logs, images, CI variables with broad scope, and client-visible configuration.

- store and issue it through a managed secret/KMS/HSM system with least-privilege identity and audited access;
- inject at runtime using the platform channel whose leakage risks you understand (agent/file/volume/API; environment variables can appear in dumps or diagnostics);
- separate environments and tenants, limit blast radius, and use short-lived dynamic credentials where supported;
- rotate on compromise, personnel/ownership change, cryptoperiod, or policy trigger; automate renewal and test overlap/rollback rather than rotating blindly and causing an outage;
- scan current code, Git history, artifacts, logs, tickets, and images; a discovered credential is revoked/reissued, not merely deleted from the latest commit.

:::deep
A Kubernetes `Secret` is base64-encoded data, not encryption by itself. Configure etcd encryption at rest, tight RBAC, namespace/service-account isolation, audit, and preferably an external secret/KMS integration for high-value material.

JWT signing-key rotation requires publishing old and new public keys under distinct `kid` values, switching the signer, retaining the old verification key through maximum token lifetime plus skew, then retiring it. Root/CA, database, webhook, PSP/GDS, and encryption-key rotations need similarly explicit dependency order and rollback.

Use envelope encryption for stored data: a data-encryption key encrypts data and a KMS/HSM-managed key encrypts the DEK. Maintain an inventory of secret owner, consumers, scope, creation/expiry, rotation method, and emergency revocation. Never log the material; log the secret identifier/version and access decision.
:::
